# ── Counterfactual: `prisoner -> person` deleted ─────────────────────────────
# Chapter 7's headline result, run against a file where the line is gone.
# These verdicts are what the world looks like WITHOUT it. They must PASS here
# and would fail against the real constitution — that contrast is the point.
:expect-pins 3

# THE FIREWALL IS DEAD. Against the constitution this rule is refused with
# "'prisoner' -> 'believe' (negative)". Here it loads, and the heretic is jailed.
:accept-scoped
all $x: person($x) & ~believe($x) -> prisoner($x).

# The floor still derives for anyone with a roster entry — the loss is not the
# floor, it is the protection around it.
? entitled(Bela, event { believe() }).
# => TRUE

# And conviction no longer confers personhood. Note Article 1 has a second route
# since release landed — `free -> person` — but that one needs somebody to write
# a fact, so conviction remains the only route into rights-bearing status that
# needs nobody's permission, and this fixture removes it.
? person(Zed).
# => FALSE
